# Copy to `.env.local`. Every variable is optional: with none of them set, the app
# runs entirely on the Phase C mock layer, which is the V1 default.
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local

# Origin of the NestJS API. GraphQL is at ${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL}/graphql; the two
# cookie routes are at ${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL}/auth.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cutover flags (config/backend.ts). One per backend slice, all off by default, so
# a half-migrated app is a working app. `1` or `true` turns one on.
#
# Independent on purpose: turning the catalog on does not require auth, and a
# problem in one slice never takes another down with it.
#
# Everything below needs the API *and* a migrated, seeded PostgreSQL:
#
#     brew services start redis          # or the compose file, if you have Docker
#     createdb -h localhost -O foodora foodora
#     cd database && bun run migrate:deploy
#     cd backend  && bun run seed:reference && bun run seed:demo
#     cd backend  && bun run build && bun run start
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Unit 0 — sign-in, registration, OTP, password reset, session restore.
# Social sign-in has no backend mutation yet and refuses while this is on.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_AUTH=0

# Unit 1 — cuisines, categories, the vendor directory, restaurant detail, menus.
#
# `next build` does NOT need the API: every catalog route renders on demand, so the
# build never reads one. Serving them does — a request with no API behind it fails
# loudly rather than rendering an empty catalog.
#
# One trade to know about: category tiles come from the API, so the CMS editor's
# device-local overrides for them are inert until the CMS module lands.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_CATALOG=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resilience (Unit 2). Both apply to live reads; neither affects the mock layer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How long one GraphQL operation may take before it is abandoned. Without a
# deadline, a Server Component waiting on an unreachable API holds its segment
# open forever: the shell has already streamed, so you get a header, a footer and
# a permanent gap. The API's own budget is 30s, but nobody waits 30s for a
# restaurant list.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Whether a failed catalog READ falls back to the bundled mock layer. On unless
# set to 0. It keeps a browse page working when the API is down — and it hides
# breakage, which is why every fallback logs to the console and why writes never
# fall back. Set to 0 in CI, or whenever the question is "is the live path working".
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_FALLBACK=1

# Unit 2 — the server-side basket: add, update quantity, remove, clear.
#
# Independent of auth: a basket predates a customer, so an anonymous visitor's cart
# persists against a `guestKey` this browser generates (`lib/cart-key.ts`).
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_CART=0

# Unit 3 — checkout: the basket priced server-side, and the order.
#
# The one flag with a prerequisite: it REQUIRES `NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_CART=1`, because
# the server prices the *server's* basket. With the cart still in Zustand only, an
# order would be priced from an empty one — so `services/orders.ts` takes the live
# path only when both are on.
#
# It also requires a signed-in customer, which is a property of the operation rather
# than of the flag: `orders.userId` is the only owner column there is. A basket built
# before signing in is adopted onto the account at checkout, so nothing is lost.
#
# Tracking and the kitchen board follow in Unit 5.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_ORDERS=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not implemented yet — the flags exist so the shape of the cutover is visible,
# and each one lands with its unit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Unit 6 — rider assignment, the delivery job, the handover OTP.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_DELIVERY=0

# Unit 7 — the notification feed and its preferences.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_NOTIFICATIONS=0
